128
00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:43.960
the as the major iteration
that you want people

129
00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:47.520
to remember to distribute
and to access in the future.

130
00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:48.760
So maybe you

131
00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:51.840
you submitted initially to a journal,
but then you caught a bunch of bugs

132
00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:54.840
and then you fix them,
and that's the version you want out there.

133
00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:59.320
So having an idea of what that version is
can be really useful.

134
00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:05.240
In Richard Holden's example,
he initially envisioned his work as a

135
00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:10.000
