137
00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:15.920
That evolved then,
as he was working on his MFA

138
00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:18.400
into a digital hypertext narrative.

139
00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:21.640
So he has held on to those early copies.

140
00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:25.760
You can see the text there that Dean
is showing you in the original journal,

141
00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:29.560
as he was self-publishing on CD-ROMs.

142
00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:29.800
Right.

143
00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:34.320
So he had these self-published CD ROMs
where he was putting early versions up.

144
00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:38.320
Then he finally had what he sees
as the canonical copy

145
00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:40.120
published by Eastgate Systems.

146
00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:43.320
And so now that he kind of knows
that we're able to define

147
00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:46.320
which one he says
is the authorial version.

148
00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:50.280
Speaking of versions, though,
you want to save those old versions

149
00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:51.480
as Richard Holton did.

150
00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:53.600
He has that text narrative.

151
00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:57.840
He has these early beta copies
that he was distributing at conferences

152
00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:01.920
into his his professors at the time
and his dissertation committees.

153
00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:06.360
So holding on to those you can see, there
we go, Dean, showing some of those

154
00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:11.240
early versions, and we'll talk later
about how we define versions the next.

155
00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:13.200
So you can have an idea about that too.

156
00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:15.160
Keeping on

157
00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:19.480
to these helps tell the story of how
the narrative traveled, how it changed,

158
00:08:19.480 --> 00:08:22.480
and how the technology was driving
the story as well.

159
00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:25.960
Companion material also.

160
00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:30.560
So if you have anything that accompanies
your artwork like physical media

161
00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:35.040
such as maybe a zine or a book,
or promotional materials

162
00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:39.640
like posters, announcement cards,
those are really important to save too,

163
00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:42.280
because they tell the story
not just of your work,

164
00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:45.280
but also of the field
and what was happening in the field.

165
00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:49.040
Denise holding up examples of Richard
Holden's notes.

166
00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:52.560
These are the notes that he was taking
as he was figuring out

167
00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:56.440
what the hyper textual structure of this
piece was going to look like,

168
00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:00.640
how the lexia were going to lay it out,
be laid out where the links were.

169
00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:03.960
This is a work
that's really connected to its structure

170
00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:05.400
and the number of links,

171
00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:07.720
and where those are going
and how they connect to each other.

172
00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:09.000
We're incredibly important.

173
00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:13.120
So having that documentation
actually became incredibly critical

174
00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:17.840
when preserving, reconstructing
and trying to keep that work accessible

175
00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:22.320
for future generations, because we knew
what the artist had made decisions by

176
00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:25.520
so we could inform that
in preservation processes.

177
00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:28.480
We also found that contracts have been

178
00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:32.280
incredibly useful documents to hold on to,
not just to protect yourself.

179
00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:35.680
Of course, and to say, hey,
I did do this thing, but also helps us

180
00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:41.360
negotiate metadata errors like date
collaborators or publication details

181
00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:45.360
that maybe have gotten lost in time
or were misprinted or miss distributed.

182
00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:50.880
The big takeaway here
is that the archives that you save

183
00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:54.040
and what you save tell your story.

184
00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:58.040
It is our human nature
to fill a void of information

185
00:09:58.040 --> 00:10:01.920
with speculation, and with what
information and knowledge we can find.

186
00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:05.760
So keeping
your archives gives you an opportunity

187
00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:08.920
to have a voice in
how that story is constructed.

188
00:10:09.080 --> 00:10:10.520
Just like Deanie was able to

189
00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:14.840
then reconstruct what is left of glide
