And Holly's going to kick it off
with documenting processes.

215
00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:36.120
Yeah.

216
00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:38.400
So this this ties in really nicely
into what

217
00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:41.440
we were just talking about,
which is you know, what do you save.

218
00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:45.200
Because a lot of those things, you know,
are deeply connected to your process.

219
00:11:45.480 --> 00:11:49.080
So first, it's important to emphasize
that we as the archivists

220
00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:54.080
and preservationists of the next,
take the stance that we do not want

221
00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:59.080
a preservation or archival process
to just prescribe how you make your art,

222
00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:03.560
make your art, how and where
and why you want to make it.

223
00:12:03.560 --> 00:12:07.240
If you want to make your art on a platform
that is dead

224
00:12:07.240 --> 00:12:10.280
or is going to die,
that's part of the piece in itself.

225
00:12:10.280 --> 00:12:12.400
Don't stop doing that.

226
00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:15.400
You don't make it however you want,

227
00:12:15.560 --> 00:12:19.800
but also consider
documenting its existence.

228
00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:22.080
And there are a lot of ways
that you can do that.

229
00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:25.640
A lot of electronic literature also isn't
web based, even though much of it is.

230
00:12:25.720 --> 00:12:28.680
A lot of us make installations
that are digital pieces.

231
00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:31.080
A lot of us do performances
that are fleeting.

232
00:12:31.080 --> 00:12:33.080
Net proms and platforms die.

233
00:12:33.080 --> 00:12:38.520
So documenting what's happening around
that lets us know that it's still existed

234
00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:41.600
without being constrained
by the preservation process

235
00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:44.600
of only creating open source software,
you know?

236
